    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The ProForm Pro 9000 is the entry point into automatic incline and decline training, capping out at a more modest 12% but still offering real terrain variety for shoppers who aren't ready for a four-figure incline trainer.",
    description:
      "We included the Pro 9000 specifically to represent the lower end of what still counts as genuine incline training rather than a token 3-4% tilt. Its -3% to 12% automatic range is modest next to the steeper machines in this roundup, but it's still a meaningfully wider spread than most sub-$2,000 treadmills offer, and the automatic adjustment is designed to track iFit's guided hill workouts. The 22-inch HD touchscreen is a genuine surprise at this price point, giving trail video content a large display that makes incline sessions more immersive than the spec sheet alone would suggest. A 3.5 CHP motor is adequate for 12% grade walking and light jogging, and at this price the drivetrain and frame are tuned more for moderate work than sustained high-intensity hill sprints, so expect a less substantial build than the Sole or NordicTrack Commercial machines. At a typical price of <strong>$1,499-$1,699</strong>, it's the most accessible way to get real automatic incline and decline into a home gym, and for someone building toward more serious hill training without committing to a specialist machine yet, it's a sensible first step.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
