        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Incline Treadmills",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-incline-treadmills",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What's a good incline percentage for beginners?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most beginners get strong results starting around 3-6% incline at an easy walking pace, then building toward 8-10% as fitness improves. Jumping straight to 15% or higher before your calves, Achilles tendons, and cardiovascular system have adapted is a common cause of early soreness and frustration.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does treadmill incline burn more calories than speed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Incline is generally the more joint-friendly way to raise calorie burn, since even a modest grade increases effort substantially without the impact forces that come with running faster. Many exercise physiologists consider a brisk incline walk comparable in calorie burn to a much faster flat jog, with less stress on the knees and hips.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can incline training replace outdoor hill running?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It gets remarkably close, especially on treadmills with true automatic incline and decline like the ones in this guide, but it isn't a perfect substitute. Treadmill inclines are precise and consistent, while outdoor hills add uneven terrain, wind resistance, and stabilizer-muscle demands that a belt can't fully replicate.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a steep incline of 30% or higher safe for daily use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most healthy adults, yes, in moderation, but 30%+ grades are intense enough that we'd recommend easing into them a few times a week rather than daily, especially early on. Anyone with knee, hip, or cardiovascular concerns should check with a doctor before regularly training at extreme inclines.",
          },
        },
