          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do all incline treadmills automatically adjust the grade?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Some machines require you to press up and down buttons to manually step the incline in increments, while others adjust automatically in real time to match a streamed workout's terrain. Automatic adjustment is more convenient for guided hill training but isn't required if you're comfortable programming your own intervals.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much more expensive are high-incline treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Expect a real premium once you get above roughly 15-20% incline capability, since it requires a stronger incline motor, reinforced frame, and often a longer deck. Across the market, machines with a true 30-40% range typically start well above $1,500 and can reach $3,000 or more at the top of the category.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-04-22",
      dateModified: "2026-04-22",
      publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
